#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "a669adb9-c12e-520d-994b-cddf4f9fedb6" :question/math-academy-id "e-233" :question/problem "Maria is flying a kite $K$ at an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to the horizontal. The kite's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the kite. If the kite is moving towards Maria with a speed of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ what is the speed of the kite's shadow?\n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\n![](images/1a/1a00ce10db2d8a0fa292bd3ca67744e7cbaafcf1861cbbdc049832d287926d28.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69402f16f81a6653e1fdd313c314ac72db7f5514501af1ac147b5e499b305233.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between María and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between Maria and the kite.\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm dt} = -5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}$ (negative because the distance between Maria and the kite is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot (-5) = -2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $2.5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"}] :question/id #uuid "7c740ffe-3391-52cc-b8fd-7fb9a54e58cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-234" :question/problem "Yurii is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $45\\,\\textrm{m}.$ The angle of elevation of the kite $\\theta$ decreases at a rate of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ radians per second. At what rate is the altitude of the kite is decreasing at the moment when $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}?$\n\n![](images/83/83f63ada35f2d9f64cd4b5013ccfcc86a97e48c7d19b49c403d21222a2e14d7b.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/04/04784c267488ef2073c526aed683e86ca0a00d00af64eb994ce596c0cce629d9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Yurii. The quantity $z=45$ meters does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{45} \\\\ y & =45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =45(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate that the kite is losing altitude is $\\dfrac{3\\pi\\sqrt3}{2} \\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"}] :question/id #uuid "ceddd310-5fe4-5b55-8210-991ff22c66b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7349" :question/problem "Peter is standing on the bank of a river. He observes a speed boat $B$ going downstream at a constant distance of $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the bank. If the boat moves at a constant speed of $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$ parallel to the bank, at what rate is the angle $\\theta,$ between the observer's line of sight to the boat and the bank, decreasing when $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6?$ Assume the angle of elevation is decreasing at a **positive** rate, and do not round the answer.\n\n![](images/ab/abf063d149ae14c11b9ca62208a4390b32ae191418c2e0fe77f6038479fd5625.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268fe4b1-9904-5930-be01-e227a74d03fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523331} {:answer/id #uuid "5854e880-7428-5210-a058-b6505ae99e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523434} {:answer/id #uuid "7af3fffa-7393-5026-be78-1811f8567119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523507} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f5b7c-0be1-525e-a404-ab3cebcbea47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523615} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a95e63-c6a3-5bfa-b132-f79f7862b533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f5b7c-0be1-525e-a404-ab3cebcbea47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523615} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f038ecd-566c-52cf-a265-319e6fd62a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f624216d-33b0-5bec-b567-6dca0950cf8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-121345" :question/problem "![](images/96/96b70c61e20b693a29a125b5f03243a75d3bfd0aeb051a2f6f74525cfdb3da78.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and flies at a constant angle of ${45}^{∘}$ to the horizontal. How fast is the drone gaining altitude if its speed is $42\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/40/40e0ae889d241c45464492ba3842d586effe528ab93b3554f6dbfb01d215c308.png)\n\nLet $y$ be the drone's altitude and $z$ be the actual distance between the drone and its take-off point, as shown below.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=42\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 42=21\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6450d8-f3a6-54ab-9544-7b06bd9a9110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523555} {:answer/id #uuid "9c21f96f-97a0-5e7e-b2ad-fbd0cbe2b848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523580} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6a3b6c-1c88-50bf-8e79-4cc6e65bfc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523618} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be432a-fe44-5397-8f82-aad61839983d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523724} {:answer/id #uuid "f6424a43-72c3-5b4d-aca6-aa3f6a38edb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6450d8-f3a6-54ab-9544-7b06bd9a9110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523555} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f706d85-4abb-551b-ac2a-41285f5f5b00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c70a238b-9801-5892-a5eb-17f62e1925f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121348" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e96676aca66b99fd1e080d6c0cbbe79a5197125a3bcc8f04e67ca406947af10.png)\n\nA bullet $B$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ from the horizontal. Given that the bullet's horizontal speed is $300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ calculate the actual speed of the bullet in the direction it is traveling." :question/worked-solution "![](images/fe/fea05a36487813579d4633d9134bdd1d8c4d4bef4e9f57ba997b7b8715d46f32.png)\n\nLet $x$ denote the horizontal distance from the bullet's projection to the cannon, and $z$ be the distance between the bullet and the cannon, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=300\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2\\cdot 300=600.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the plane is $600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523490} {:answer/id #uuid "91812201-758f-5436-91d6-fd93a8c56e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{18}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523559} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2721fb-1174-5306-a632-bbe89f079355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523575} {:answer/id #uuid "b03d0bbd-2440-5230-9491-512dd5859d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523624} {:answer/id #uuid "b75d5654-537e-53c4-bc29-9857e0f9d0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523490} :answer-field/id #uuid "da00c2cd-b023-5b35-af39-4086a9fdeebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-121763" :question/problem "![](images/22/2264aa2a4f3d33b04190608ab638604dbe09dfd278035b2b764aa3d85d947624.png)\n\nGreta is flying a kite $K.$ The kite is  $150\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and moving horizontally towards Greta at a speed of $10\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d15c54-d685-5399-aff3-46571533a8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523266} {:answer/id #uuid "615761ef-1628-5799-88a8-d90bf4101a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523450} {:answer/id #uuid "70755312-5bed-52eb-a77d-bfe44df9ed49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523485} {:answer/id #uuid "88970476-3499-5b1f-b573-1a1ad887eb08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523540} {:answer/id #uuid "dc57e585-10be-5f7b-846b-54fad41e6666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05d15c54-d685-5399-aff3-46571533a8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523266} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c6c883-58d6-591a-815a-6edbc95ab9ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "419bbacd-9dab-5629-96fc-19c329c4d054" :question/math-academy-id "q-2910" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a51d0d8ef1be2e7fc06aa34fe1440e77a79d8bee654703c1e931332390741c32.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ ladder leans against the side of a building, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. Given that the foot of the ladder $F$ is being pulled away from the building at the rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/s}$, at what rate is $\\theta $ decreasing at the moment when the top of the ladder $T$ is $12\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7b/7b923b76e90d665a722643684ce94fff50500a7be8b70aa821410a7969e4bb79.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the foot of the ladder to the wall of the building, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the ladder. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the ladder's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=12$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{26} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\cdot \\frac{13}{12}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\theta $ is *decreasing* at a rate of $\\frac{1}{24}$ radians per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\csc^{2}(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\csc^{2}(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523678} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8015e3-4339-5833-a2d3-370dba776e3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-314884" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf0aa0508025b800bb0707b74ffdc89b08052ad6fced401b8071d7ad741f8e0a.png)\n\nA lighthouse $L$ shines a beam that hits the surface of the sea at point $B.$ The light is mounted at a height of $10\\,\\text{m}$ above the water. As the beam is tilted downward, the angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the beam and the point $B$ is increasing at a rate of $0.12$ radians per second. What is the speed of the light spot as a function of $\\theta ?$ \n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\nThe speed of the light spot is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523658} :answer-field/id #uuid "d870faa5-187d-5160-bfd1-02d58f3d6875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343159" :question/problem "![](images/55/55e96238e8263a1744c117bad09d1b11947f70a8bf1b837c52af18cf2e244ab7.png)\n\nA park ranger $P$ stands $900\\,\\text{ft}$ away from the base of a lookout tower $T$ to track a drone $D$ that is ascending vertically. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the ranger and the drone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ radians per second. At what rate is the drone gaining altitude at the moment when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone’s altitude is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa527cb-58ec-5808-8a78-746b3d613e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fa527cb-58ec-5808-8a78-746b3d613e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523353} :answer-field/id #uuid "af64dd03-c64b-5792-87c8-369472ee1a77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "017bbabc-f897-558b-b361-4f91d645fa28" :question/math-academy-id "q-343195" :question/problem "![](images/22/2242cf493c6307d8c6f2dac778efe6f89ca4ba6f04203940076360613c8dc9b0.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ support pole leans against a vertical wall, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. The base of the pole $B$ is being pulled toward the wall at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ At what rate is $\\theta $ increasing at the moment when the top of the pole $T$ is $5\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e08cddcde0d709ebe455b8d8935174955798b877f054dab0de74d06035accc91.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the base of the pole to the wall, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the pole and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the pole. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the pole's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=5$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-2$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{13} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523470} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea67d905-4e13-5fb0-9a4b-c810c57cacc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343202" :question/problem "![](images/30/305398b239bbc784fb5e5a1ec7ef2020a19a033dcda150e84f9c4a2a72a96adf.png)\n\nA lifeguard $L$ is watching a weather balloon $W$ rise vertically from a point $S$ on the beach. The point $S$ is $10\\,\\text{m}$ away from the lifeguard. If the balloon is rising at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the lifeguard and the balloon is increasing at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0552b87f-9e0e-5fad-9e7d-9912b470a176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523263} {:answer/id #uuid "105c6107-f0ae-5027-9f7f-377577430bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523289} {:answer/id #uuid "36356009-3cdd-5659-8ec9-cead4b18b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523368} {:answer/id #uuid "b7643b39-11ad-58a4-92de-90e602e97f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523638} {:answer/id #uuid "cf65a21a-c75d-54d3-b6d0-20ebce85da28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0552b87f-9e0e-5fad-9e7d-9912b470a176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523263} :answer-field/id #uuid "5479eb5b-fe35-5bb6-87a9-6ac02c1066b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7145361b-50f5-5e70-a538-4868d130565b" :question/math-academy-id "q-53161" :question/problem "![](images/06/0640883625711f20e1441946461abd0f352ed1418065b325f48ea2b709fef230.png)\n\nA cableway system is descending at an angle of ${45}^{∘},$ and the speed of its cabin $C$ is $10\\,\\text{mi/h}.$ The cabin casts a shadow $S$ vertically downwards to the ground. Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, find the rate at which the distance between the cableway's shadow to the landing point $L$ is decreasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/4d/4d2a9ab6bc60aa5733be4dc29aea2b03d16edbebed371963b795297a4c79a676.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance from the cableway's shadow to the landing point, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the cabin, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,\\text{mi/h}$ (negative because the distance between the cableway's shadow and the landing point is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-10)=-5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal distance is decreasing at a rate of $5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5ba0a7-0f58-5b5c-822b-751a3fca36dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523352} {:answer/id #uuid "329a50a1-1867-5bec-a6de-7ff85577816f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523358} {:answer/id #uuid "66fcb494-c0d9-5ffb-979e-79b874aa5657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523465} {:answer/id #uuid "a65dd8fb-bbd1-58b7-ac02-a42d4c6ccf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523604} {:answer/id #uuid "a74ae6ab-9a62-5384-a6ec-5a1fa233a9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a65dd8fb-bbd1-58b7-ac02-a42d4c6ccf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523604} :answer-field/id #uuid "4203119c-e32d-5b16-b15e-33670897cf42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a3baf57-ae5a-55ac-b1d2-8d2d13d9875d" :question/math-academy-id "q-53195" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa0da0d113d1077b1a394f2f45fe839f8845d1bbe544e2497582df18e079c52a.png)\n\nA drone $D$ is approaching a landing point $L$ on the ground at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to the horizontal. The drone's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the drone. If the drone is approaching the ground with a speed of $6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ what is the speed of the drone's shadow?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](images/9c/9cb94ea5cab43d48da6b836026d302504d7e63fe1260aa81a2b577436b6b4e98.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between the landing point and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the drone, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ (negative because the distance between the landing point and the drone is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-6)=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023efee2-ebd9-53fc-8796-286eca083d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523254} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3a66d2-ada2-57e6-bc94-a83355eb8c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523351} {:answer/id #uuid "4919ab2f-fbd2-574b-9eda-ce5259c7401d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523404} {:answer/id #uuid "9828920c-e8c5-59e0-8330-a343e0ffcdf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523571} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4333fa-407f-57e6-8e89-7c8e98ab48ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f3a66d2-ada2-57e6-bc94-a83355eb8c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce76d2c6-da78-53ff-8c24-134a2e050d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e817aceb-adb1-5216-bfe1-e48f45d3b767" :question/math-academy-id "q-53196" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d486581ea48bc96adc1269fbf40d1d71a547e92977f71e3a74336a147ef9b222.png)\n\nA potato $P$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians from the horizontal. The horizontal distance the projectile travels increases at a rate of $18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ If the angle of elevation does not change, what is the speed of the potato?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8b/8b5af04f58fe65b46adaae47d97b857d1e8765122a811179cf25ba22733e6415.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the horizontal distance from the potato to the cannon and $z$ be the actual distance between them, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 18=12\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the potato is $12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31679908-2281-5375-8e4f-ab234176aa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523356} {:answer/id #uuid "5aae7333-f8d9-59d1-980d-c32006c2344a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523438} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3a5aba-80e0-512f-9adf-de9315810712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523523} {:answer/id #uuid "a505aa4e-e343-59d5-ac42-7ab4c259cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523601} {:answer/id #uuid "a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c764bd62-dac9-5c30-b3ea-6e5444aa2fdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83678" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a37ae024b0472dfc38ecff01b898d8d3ed2384d90acf432d747efee1568520e.png)\n\nA car $C$ drives toward a $108\\,\\text{m}$ high building $B$ following a straight path. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the car and the top of the building $T$ is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ radians per second. What is the speed of the car when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi (\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0655097e-96d7-5165-8639-97ef17c063f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523268} {:answer/id #uuid "5bcdd1a8-0496-5b15-ae0c-0eae44361812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523441} {:answer/id #uuid "8be20d7e-4faa-5503-9ffc-8df5111f4c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523543} {:answer/id #uuid "d14ce059-5af9-5b4b-a55f-442b30db578a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523695} {:answer/id #uuid "e53e56f8-da78-5344-b9a6-bc6a67f4c209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0655097e-96d7-5165-8639-97ef17c063f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523268} :answer-field/id #uuid "b235a92b-fff1-5573-96d9-db640270afae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8512c354-4d05-539a-b5eb-ac69014916bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-83679" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2e2666baf2931dc4e0d8ba097a364b7b7a1bba75e65c8d802a60180e9a94854.png)\n\nNeah is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $112\\,\\text{ft}.$ The angle of elevation $\\theta $ of the kite is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{28}$ radians per second. Find, as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the kite's altitude is increasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/8c/8c3c9e40f56d0b269562c37fd2d87c7e8f0394c8f55dca596e5e751f5e96157b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Neah. The quantity $z=112\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{112} \\\\ y & =112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{28}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\pi }{28} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the kite is gaining altitude is $4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c27343-2ffe-5ef0-95ae-6705c24c596c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523360} {:answer/id #uuid "7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523488} {:answer/id #uuid "86eef243-02da-54f6-8ff5-46912fa718e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523537} {:answer/id #uuid "9adf3a93-53a6-5212-9c75-fbcceecb6e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523577} {:answer/id #uuid "be8f074c-4255-5799-bd18-aae998046419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523488} :answer-field/id #uuid "0708b89d-7f7a-5637-8493-1ca6f099288c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86335" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce513880934a99b289c22f734e8a455c3534bddd911968cd06b7fe88f65441aa.png)\n\nA hot air balloon $B$ is flying horizontally to the observer $O$ at a constant height of $300\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the balloon and an observer is increasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the balloon when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1493380f-c708-5ae2-ad63-0ba124cacc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523297} {:answer/id #uuid "368ef356-d80b-56d6-b93f-d58e6e4b200b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523370} {:answer/id #uuid "69614c5f-1731-5732-8a72-f2c31ba8aa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523471} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbc4914-cd92-5ba6-9302-86b96db75dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523579} {:answer/id #uuid "dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523719} :answer-field/id #uuid "d828883d-a3eb-5584-b5bd-f62f8f377e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86352" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f4638863dbc6bdfbff00d20d2153bb73eb7779719bbc7235d730539cb92329e.png)\n\nAngela observes that a drone is flying horizontally at a constant height of $5\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. If the drone is flying in the opposite direction from Angela's location at a speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ decreases at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second."}]}
